From 6668c5d85af88b79b7c6a4b8950525367e034609 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Alexander=20Thei=C3=9Fen?= <alex@theissen.io>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 17:32:26 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 07/47] FEXCore: Write back MMX and upper YMM per instruction
 in writable code

MMX registers, and the upper halves of the YMM registers when the host
has no 256-bit SVE, are cached like DF and stayed in the register cache
until the end of the block. A store into a page of the running compiled
unit is inline SMC: the fault handler rebuilds the guest state from the
SRA host registers and the context and steps the faulting instruction
again in a block of its own. A value that an instruction had loaded into
one of them before the store was lost there, and the rest of the block
ran with the older one.

Inline SMC needs a store into a writable executable range that the
running unit was decoded from, such as JIT output or a packed image
section, and the decoder knows which ranges those are. A unit can reach
past such a range into code that isn't writable, a stub in a writable
page that jumps into the image's text for one, and the store then hits
the stub's page while a block of the text is running. So when any code
of a unit lies in a writable range, all of its blocks now write those
cache entries back at every instruction boundary too, like DF: only
after an instruction changed one, and it stays cached. Other units are
compiled as before.

In writable code each MMX write gets two stores, the value and its
0xFFFF exponent, instead of two per register at the end of the block,
which makes a loop of independent MMX instructions about two thirds
slower there. A 256-bit AVX instruction gets one 128-bit store, and AVX
loops get faster: the next iteration's load of an upper half no longer
waits for a paired store from the end of the previous one.

Not covered: a guest exception in code outside writable ranges that a
handler continues also rebuilds the state mid-block, so MMX values and
upper YMM halves written earlier in that block are still lost there. The
x87 stack optimization pass keeps the x87 stack values, TOP and tags
until the end of the block, so those are lost on inline SMC too. Only
the ranges the decoder read code from count, so a writable page that a
forward jump skips inside the span of the unit is not counted, which
needs a writable page between read-only ones in one section or in
anonymous memory. The disk cache keys a unit by the writability of its
entry range only, so a cached unit can be used with the other setting
when the ranges it reaches past that one differ in writability from the
run that stored it.

Code that was ever writable and executable keeps the write-back after
it is made read-only, since the invalidation tracker keeps such ranges
in its writable list when their protection changes to read and execute.

CodeSizeValidation now reports its code as not writable, like the code
of most programs, so the InstructionCountCI listings keep showing the
usual code generation. The new InlineSMC_MMX_AVX and InlineSMC_MMX
tests run from the writable test harness mapping and cover the other
case: they load MMX and YMM values in a block that then stores into its
own page.

